      "worked": "<strong>Find the decimal value of binary 101101.</strong><br>Write the positional weights above each bit, starting from 0 on the right:<br><code>bit&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;:&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; 0&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; 0&nbsp;&nbsp; 1</code><br><code>weight:&nbsp;32&nbsp; 16&nbsp;&nbsp; 8&nbsp;&nbsp; 4&nbsp;&nbsp; 2&nbsp;&nbsp; 1</code><br>Now add only the weights that sit under a 1:<br>32 + 8 + 4 + 1 = <strong>45</strong><br><br><strong>Same method for hexadecimal 2AF:</strong><br>2 × 16^2 = 2 × 256 = 512<br>A (=10) × 16^1 = 10 × 16 = 160<br>F (=15) × 16^0 = 15 × 1 = 15<br>512 + 160 + 15 = <strong>687</strong>",
