      "examAnswer": "A number system is a method of representing numbers using a fixed set of symbols, and it is characterised by its base or radix, which is the number of distinct digits it uses. In a positional number system, the value contributed by a digit is the digit multiplied by the base raised to the power of its position, where positions are numbered from zero at the least significant digit. The decimal system uses base ten, the binary system base two, the octal system base eight and the hexadecimal system base sixteen. Computers use the binary system because electronic devices have two stable states, while octal and hexadecimal are used as compact human-readable forms of binary, since one octal digit represents three bits and one hexadecimal digit represents four bits.",
