      "realLife": "A MAC address on your Wi-Fi router looks like <code>3C:5A:B4:0F:22:9D</code>. That is 48 bits of hardware identity, but nobody wants to read 48 ones and zeros — so it is shown as 12 hex digits, four bits each.",
      "worked": "<strong>ONE number, 45, converted into every base.</strong><br><br><strong>Step 1 — Decimal 45 to binary (repeated division by 2):</strong><br><code>45 ÷ 2 = 22, remainder 1</code>  ← LSB<br><code>22 ÷ 2 = 11, remainder 0</code><br><code>11 ÷ 2 =  5, remainder 1</code><br><code>&nbsp;5 ÷ 2 =  2, remainder 1</code><br><code>&nbsp;2 ÷ 2 =  1, remainder 0</code><br><code>&nbsp;1 ÷ 2 =  0, remainder 1</code>  ← MSB<br>Read the remainders <strong>bottom to top</strong>: <strong>(45)₁₀ = (101101)₂</strong><br><em>Check:</em> 32 + 8 + 4 + 1 = 45 ✓<br><br><strong>Step 2 — Decimal 45 to octal (divide by 8):</strong><br><code>45 ÷ 8 = 5, remainder 5</code><br><code>&nbsp;5 ÷ 8 = 0, remainder 5</code><br>Reading upwards: <strong>(45)₁₀ = (55)₈</strong> &nbsp; <em>Check:</em> 5×8 + 5 = 45 ✓<br><br><strong>Step 3 — Decimal 45 to hexadecimal (divide by 16):</strong><br><code>45 ÷ 16 = 2, remainder 13 → D</code><br><code>&nbsp;2 ÷ 16 = 0, remainder 2</code><br>Reading upwards: <strong>(45)₁₀ = (2D)₁₆</strong> &nbsp; <em>Check:</em> 2×16 + 13 = 45 ✓<br><br><strong>Step 4 — Binary to octal by grouping 3 bits from the right:</strong><br><code>101101 → 101 | 101 → 5 | 5 → (55)₈</code> ✓<br><br><strong>Step 5 — Binary to hexadecimal by grouping 4 bits from the right:</strong><br><code>101101 → pad to 00101101 → 0010 | 1101 → 2 | D → (2D)₁₆</code> ✓<br><br><strong>Step 6 — Going backwards.</strong> Hex 2D → binary: 2 = 0010, D = 1101, so 00101101 = 101101 ✓. Octal 55 → binary: 5 = 101, 5 = 101, so 101101 ✓. Every road leads back to the same number.<br><br><strong>Bonus — a decimal fraction.</strong> Convert 0.625 to binary by multiplying by 2:<br><code>0.625 × 2 = 1.25 → carry 1</code><br><code>0.25&nbsp; × 2 = 0.5&nbsp; → carry 0</code><br><code>0.5&nbsp;&nbsp; × 2 = 1.0&nbsp; → carry 1, fraction now 0, stop</code><br>Read the carries <strong>downwards</strong>: (0.625)₁₀ = <strong>(0.101)₂</strong> &nbsp; <em>Check:</em> 0.5 + 0.125 = 0.625 ✓",
      "mistake": "Two errors cost marks every year. First, reading the remainders top-to-bottom instead of bottom-to-top — that gives you the number backwards. Second, grouping bits from the <strong>left</strong> instead of the right; grouping must always start at the LSB, and it is the leftmost group that gets padded with zeros.",
      "examAnswer": "Conversion from decimal to another base is performed by repeatedly dividing the decimal number by the target base and recording the remainders, which are then read in reverse order, from the last remainder to the first. Conversion from any base to decimal is performed by multiplying each digit by the base raised to the power of its position and summing the products. Conversion between binary and octal is achieved by grouping the binary digits into sets of three from the right, and between binary and hexadecimal by grouping into sets of four, because eight and sixteen are the third and fourth powers of two respectively. Conversion between octal and hexadecimal is therefore carried out by first converting to binary. For example, the decimal number 45 is 101101 in binary, 55 in octal and 2D in hexadecimal.",
