      "realLife": "When a game counter 'rolls over' from 255 back to 0, that is binary overflow. An 8-bit register can only hold up to 11111111 (255); add 1 more and the carry falls off the end, leaving 00000000.",
      "worked": "<strong>1. Addition: 1011 + 1101</strong> (that is 11 + 13 in decimal)<br><code>&nbsp;carry:&nbsp;1 1 1 1</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 0 1 1</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;+&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 1 0 1</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-----------</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 1 0 0 0</code><br>Column by column from the right: 1+1 = 0 carry 1 · 1+0+1 = 0 carry 1 · 0+1+1 = 0 carry 1 · 1+1+1 = 1 carry 1 · the final carry 1 is written out.<br>Answer <strong>11000 = 24</strong>, and 11 + 13 = 24 ✓<br><br><strong>2. Subtraction: 1101 − 1011</strong> (13 − 11)<br>Rightmost: 1−1 = 0. Next: 0−1 needs a borrow, so 10−1 = 1. Next column was 1, now 0, and 0−0 = 0. Leftmost: 1−1 = 0.<br>Answer <strong>0010 = 2</strong>, and 13 − 11 = 2 ✓<br><br><strong>3. Multiplication: 1011 × 101</strong> (11 × 5)<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 0 1 1</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;×&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 0 1</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-----------</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 0 1 1</code> &nbsp;(× the 1, no shift)<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 0 0 0 .</code> &nbsp;(× the 0, shifted left 1)<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;1 0 1 1 . .</code> &nbsp;(× the 1, shifted left 2)<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;-------------</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;1 1 0 1 1 1</code><br>Answer <strong>110111 = 55</strong>, and 11 × 5 = 55 ✓<br><br><strong>4. Division: 1011 ÷ 10</strong> (11 ÷ 2)<br>Long division gives quotient <strong>101</strong> and remainder <strong>1</strong>, that is 5 remainder 1 — and 11 ÷ 2 = 5 remainder 1 ✓",
