      "examAnswer": "Binary arithmetic is performed using the same positional column methods as decimal arithmetic, but with a base of two. In binary addition the rules are 0+0=0, 0+1=1, 1+0=1 and 1+1=0 with a carry of 1 into the next higher column. In binary subtraction the rules are 0−0=0, 1−0=1, 1−1=0 and 0−1=1 with a borrow taken from the next higher column. Binary multiplication uses the rules 0×0=0, 0×1=0, 1×0=0 and 1×1=1, with each partial product shifted one position to the left before the partial products are added, while binary division follows the ordinary long-division procedure. Because every partial product is either the multiplicand itself or zero, binary multiplication reduces to a sequence of shift and add operations, which is why it is simple to implement in hardware.",
