      "teacher": "Here is a real problem. A computer has no minus key — a wire cannot be 'negative on'. So how do you store −45? The answer is one of the most elegant ideas in computing: make subtraction <em>become</em> addition. Once you see how, you will never forget it.",
      "explain": "The <strong>1's complement</strong> of a binary number is obtained by flipping every bit. The <strong>2's complement</strong> is the 1's complement plus 1. Computers store negative numbers in 2's complement form, because then <strong>A − B can be computed as A + (2's complement of B)</strong> — so a single adder circuit does both addition and subtraction.",
