      "worked": "<strong>Part 1 — Find the 1's and 2's complement of +45 in 8 bits.</strong><br>+45 in binary, padded to 8 bits: <code>00101101</code><br>Flip every bit → 1's complement: <code>11010010</code><br>Add 1 → 2's complement: <code>11010010 + 1 = 11010011</code><br>So <strong>−45 = 11010011</strong> in 8-bit 2's complement.<br><em>Proof it is really −45:</em> 00101101 + 11010011 = 1 00000000. Discard the carry → 00000000 = 0. Since 45 + (−45) = 0, the representation is correct ✓<br><br><em>Shortcut check:</em> take 00101101, copy from the right up to and including the first 1 (that is the final '1'), then flip everything to its left: 0010110<u>1</u> → 1101001<u>1</u> — same answer ✓<br><br><strong>Part 2 — Subtract 45 − 20 using 2's complement (8-bit).</strong><br>45 = <code>00101101</code> &nbsp;&nbsp; 20 = <code>00010100</code><br>2's complement of 20: flip → <code>11101011</code>, add 1 → <code>11101100</code><br>Now add:<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;00101101</code><br><code>&nbsp;+&nbsp;11101100</code><br><code>&nbsp;-----------</code><br><code>&nbsp;1 00011001</code><br>A carry came out of the MSB, so <strong>discard it</strong>. Answer = <code>00011001</code> = 16 + 8 + 1 = <strong>25</strong>. And 45 − 20 = 25 ✓<br><br><strong>Part 3 — The other direction: 20 − 45.</strong><br>2's complement of 45 = <code>11010011</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;00010100</code><br><code>&nbsp;+&nbsp;11010011</code><br><code>&nbsp;-----------</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;11100111</code> &nbsp; (no carry out)<br>No carry out → the answer is <strong>negative</strong> and is in 2's complement form. Take its 2's complement to read the size: flip 11100111 → 00011000, add 1 → <code>00011001</code> = 25.<br>So the answer is <strong>−25</strong>, and 20 − 45 = −25 ✓",
      "mistake": "Do not forget the '+1' — the 1's complement alone is <strong>not</strong> the negative number. And do not read 11010011 as 211. In a signed context, an MSB of 1 means negative, so that pattern is −45, not 211. The <em>same bits</em> mean different numbers depending on whether the register is signed or unsigned.",
      "examAnswer": "The 1's complement of a binary number is obtained by replacing every 0 with 1 and every 1 with 0, and the 2's complement is obtained by adding 1 to the 1's complement. Signed numbers may be represented in signed magnitude form, in which the most significant bit indicates the sign and the remaining bits the magnitude, or in 2's complement form, which is used in practically all modern computers. The 2's complement representation has the advantage that it possesses a unique representation of zero and that subtraction can be performed using the same adder circuit as addition, since A minus B is computed as A plus the 2's complement of B, with any carry out of the most significant position being discarded. For an n-bit register, the 2's complement representation covers the range from −2^(n−1) to +2^(n−1) − 1, which for eight bits is −128 to +127.",
      "remember": "Flip = 1's complement. Flip and add one = 2's complement. Subtract by adding, then throw the last carry away.",
      "why": "Every integer variable in C, Java or Python is stored this way. It also explains a famous bug class: why an 8-bit counter at 127 jumps to −128 when you add 1.",
      "practice": "Using 8-bit 2's complement, compute 60 − 35 and 35 − 60. Show the 2's complement step each time and state clearly whether a carry came out."
    },
    {
      "title": "Digital Codes: BCD, Excess-3, Gray and ASCII",
      "teacher": "Your name is not a number. Neither is the letter 'A', or the position of a rotating shaft. Yet all of them have to be stored as 0s and 1s. The answer is a <em>code</em> — an agreed dictionary that maps things to bit patterns. Let's meet the four codes your exam cares about.",
      "explain": "A <strong>digital code</strong> is an agreed way of representing information as a fixed pattern of bits. <strong>Numeric codes</strong> like BCD and Excess-3 represent decimal digits, <strong>Gray code</strong> is designed so that only one bit changes at a time, and <strong>alphanumeric codes</strong> like ASCII represent letters, digits and symbols.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<strong>BCD (8421 code)</strong> — each decimal digit is written separately as 4 bits. 45 → 0100 0101.",
        "BCD wastes the six patterns 1010 to 1111; they are <strong>invalid</strong> in BCD.",
        "BCD is easy for humans and for digital displays, but uses more bits than pure binary.",
        "<strong>Excess-3</strong> = BCD value + 3. Digit 5 → 0101 + 0011 = 1000. It is a <strong>self-complementing</strong> code.",
        "<strong>Gray code</strong> — successive numbers differ in exactly <strong>one bit</strong>. Also called the unit-distance or reflected code.",
        "Binary → Gray: keep the MSB, then each Gray bit = XOR of the two binary bits above it.",
        "Gray → binary: keep the MSB, then each binary bit = XOR of the previous binary bit with the current Gray bit.",
        "<strong>ASCII</strong> — 7-bit alphanumeric code, 128 characters. 'A' = 65, 'a' = 97, '0' = 48, space = 32.",
        "Extended ASCII uses 8 bits (256 characters), <strong>Unicode</strong> covers every world script, and <strong>EBCDIC</strong> is an older 8-bit IBM mainframe code."
