      "worked": "<strong>1. Write decimal 45 in BCD.</strong><br>Take each digit separately: 4 → 0100, 5 → 0101<br>BCD of 45 = <strong>0100 0101</strong> (8 bits)<br>Compare with pure binary 45 = 101101 (6 bits). Different, and that is the point.<br><br><strong>2. Write decimal 45 in Excess-3.</strong><br>Digit 4 → 4 + 3 = 7 → 0111<br>Digit 5 → 5 + 3 = 8 → 1000<br>Excess-3 of 45 = <strong>0111 1000</strong><br><br><strong>3. Convert binary 1011 to Gray code.</strong><br>Rule: MSB stays; each next Gray bit = XOR of the two binary bits above it.<br><code>Binary:&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; 0&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; 1</code><br><code>Gray&nbsp;&nbsp;:&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; 0</code><br>MSB = 1 (copied) · 1⊕0 = 1 · 0⊕1 = 1 · 1⊕1 = 0<br>Answer: <strong>Gray 1110</strong><br><br><strong>4. Convert Gray 1110 back to binary.</strong><br>Rule: MSB stays; each next binary bit = previous <em>binary</em> bit ⊕ current Gray bit.<br>b3 = 1 · b2 = 1⊕1 = 0 · b1 = 0⊕1 = 1 · b0 = 1⊕0 = 1<br>Answer: <strong>binary 1011</strong> ✓ — we are back where we started.<br><br><strong>5. Write the word 'Hi' in ASCII.</strong><br>'H' = 72 = 1001000 &nbsp;&nbsp; 'i' = 105 = 1101001<br>So 'Hi' travels as <strong>1001000 1101001</strong>.",
      "mistake": "The biggest trap is treating BCD as binary. BCD 45 = 0100 0101 is <strong>not</strong> the binary number 01000101 (which is 69). In BCD you must read the nibbles as separate decimal digits. Also remember that 1010 through 1111 are illegal BCD patterns.",
      "examAnswer": "A digital code is an agreed scheme for representing information as patterns of binary digits. Binary Coded Decimal, also called the 8421 code, represents each decimal digit individually by its four-bit binary equivalent, so that the decimal number 45 is written as 0100 0101; the six combinations from 1010 to 1111 are invalid in BCD. The Excess-3 code is obtained by adding three to the BCD value of each digit and is a self-complementing code, since the 1's complement of a digit's code gives the code of its 9's complement. The Gray code, also called the unit-distance or reflected code, is arranged so that successive values differ in only one bit position, which prevents transient errors in position sensors and encoders. ASCII, the American Standard Code for Information Interchange, is a seven-bit alphanumeric code providing 128 characters, in which the letter A is 65 and the digit character zero is 48.",
      "remember": "BCD = each digit separately. Excess-3 = BCD plus 3. Gray = only one bit changes. ASCII = letters become numbers.",
      "why": "Codes are the bridge between human information and machine bits. Every file, message and sensor reading crosses that bridge, and knowing which code is in use is often the difference between readable data and garbage.",
