    {
      "title": "Error Detection Codes: Parity and Hamming",
      "teacher": "Data travelling through a wire can get hit by electrical noise, and a 0 can flip into a 1. The receiver has no idea it happened. So how do we catch a mistake we cannot see? We deliberately send a few <em>extra</em> bits — and those extra bits do all the detective work.",
      "explain": "An <strong>error detection code</strong> adds redundant bits to the data so that a corrupted message can be recognised. A <strong>parity bit</strong> detects any odd number of bit errors but cannot locate or fix them. A <strong>Hamming code</strong> adds several parity bits positioned so that they together point at the exact bit that went wrong, allowing single-bit errors to be corrected.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<strong>Redundancy</strong> = extra bits added purely for checking, carrying no new information.",
        "<strong>Even parity</strong>: the parity bit is chosen so the total number of 1s (data + parity) is <strong>even</strong>. <strong>Odd parity</strong>: chosen so that total is <strong>odd</strong>.",
        "A single parity bit <strong>detects</strong> a single-bit error but cannot say <em>where</em> it is, and cannot correct it.",
        "Parity <strong>fails</strong> on an even number of errors — two flipped bits cancel out and go unnoticed.",
        "<strong>Checksum</strong> — send the sum of all the data blocks; the receiver re-adds and compares. <strong>CRC</strong> — division by a generator polynomial, much stronger, used in networks and disks.",
        "<strong>Hamming code</strong> — an error <strong>correcting</strong> code, it locates the faulty bit.",
        "Parity bits sit at positions that are powers of 2: <strong>1, 2, 4, 8, ...</strong>; data bits fill the rest.",
        "Number of parity bits <em>r</em> for <em>m</em> data bits must satisfy <strong>2^r ≥ m + r + 1</strong>. For 4 data bits, r = 3, giving the (7,4) Hamming code.",
        "The recomputed parity bits form the <strong>syndrome</strong>; read as a binary number it is the position of the wrong bit. Syndrome 000 means no error."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "What each parity bit covers in Hamming code",
          "text": "Parity bit P1 (position 1) checks every position whose number has bit 0 set: positions 1, 3, 5, 7. P2 (position 2) checks positions whose number has bit 1 set: 2, 3, 6, 7. P4 (position 4) checks positions 4, 5, 6, 7. Every data position is covered by a unique combination of parity bits — and that unique combination is exactly what identifies it when something goes wrong."
        },
        {
          "title": "The syndrome — how the code points at the error",
          "text": "At the receiver, each parity check is recomputed. If a check passes, write 0; if it fails, write 1. Writing these results as C4 C2 C1 gives a binary number. If it is 000 the data is clean. If it is anything else, that number is the <em>position</em> of the corrupted bit — simply flip that bit and the data is repaired."
        },
        {
          "title": "Detection versus correction",
          "text": "Detection only tells you 'something is wrong, please resend'. Correction repairs the data on the spot without asking again. Correction always costs more extra bits. So the choice depends on the situation: a network can just ask for a resend, but a spacecraft eight light-minutes away really cannot."
        }
      ],
      "realLife": "Your Aadhaar number and your bank card number both end in a check digit computed from the others. Type one digit wrong and the website rejects it instantly, before contacting any server. That is exactly the parity idea, working in decimal.",
      "worked": "<strong>Part 1 — Parity bits for the data 1011001.</strong><br>Count the 1s: 1, 1, 1, 1 → <strong>four</strong> ones, which is even.<br><strong>Even parity:</strong> the count is already even, so the parity bit is <strong>0</strong> → transmitted as <code>1011001 0</code><br><strong>Odd parity:</strong> we need an odd total, so the parity bit is <strong>1</strong> → transmitted as <code>1011001 1</code><br><br><strong>Part 2 — Build the (7,4) Hamming code for the data 1011 (even parity).</strong><br>Step 1 — lay out 7 positions. Positions 1, 2, 4 are parity bits; positions 3, 5, 6, 7 take the four data bits in order:<br><code>Position:&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; 2&nbsp;&nbsp; 3&nbsp;&nbsp; 4&nbsp;&nbsp; 5&nbsp;&nbsp; 6&nbsp;&nbsp; 7</code><br><code>Bit&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; :&nbsp; P1&nbsp; P2&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; P4&nbsp; 0&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp; 1</code><br><br>Step 2 — compute each parity bit for <em>even</em> parity:<br>P1 covers positions 1,3,5,7 → data bits there are 1, 0, 1 → two 1s, already even → <strong>P1 = 0</strong><br>P2 covers positions 2,3,6,7 → data bits there are 1, 1, 1 → three 1s, odd → <strong>P2 = 1</strong><br>P4 covers positions 4,5,6,7 → data bits there are 0, 1, 1 → two 1s, already even → <strong>P4 = 0</strong><br><br>Step 3 — the transmitted 7-bit codeword:<br><code>Position: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7</code><br><code>Codeword: 0 1 1 0 0 1 1</code><br><br><strong>Part 3 — Now suppose bit 5 gets corrupted in transit.</strong><br>Received: <code>0 1 1 0 <u>1</u> 1 1</code><br>Recheck each parity group (write 1 if the check FAILS):<br>C1 (positions 1,3,5,7) = 0,1,1,1 → three 1s, odd → check fails → <strong>C1 = 1</strong><br>C2 (positions 2,3,6,7) = 1,1,1,1 → four 1s, even → check passes → <strong>C2 = 0</strong><br>C4 (positions 4,5,6,7) = 0,1,1,1 → three 1s, odd → check fails → <strong>C4 = 1</strong><br>Syndrome = C4 C2 C1 = <strong>101 = 5</strong> in decimal.<br>So <strong>bit 5 is the wrong one</strong>. Flip it back from 1 to 0 and the original codeword 0110011 is restored ✓<br>The code found the error <em>and</em> fixed it, without asking the sender for anything.",
      "mistake": "Two classic errors. First, the syndrome is read as <strong>C4 C2 C1</strong>, with C1 as the least significant bit — reversing the order points at the wrong position. Second, students claim a single parity bit can correct errors. It cannot. It only announces that something is wrong, and it stays silent if <em>two</em> bits flip.",
      "examAnswer": "Error detection codes add redundant bits to transmitted data so that corruption can be recognised by the receiver. The simplest such scheme is the parity bit, in which one extra bit is appended so that the total number of 1s in the message is even for even parity or odd for odd parity; a single parity bit can detect any odd number of bit errors but cannot locate or correct them, and it fails entirely when an even number of bits are corrupted. The Hamming code is an error correcting code in which parity bits are placed at positions that are powers of two, namely 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on, with each parity bit checking a distinct group of positions. At the receiver the parity checks are recomputed to form a syndrome; if the syndrome is zero the data is correct, and otherwise the value of the syndrome gives the position of the erroneous bit, which can then be inverted to correct the data. For m data bits, the number of parity bits r must satisfy 2^r ≥ m + r + 1.",
      "remember": "Parity says 'something is wrong'. Hamming says 'bit number 5 is wrong'. Parity bits live at positions 1, 2, 4, 8.",
      "why": "Every hard disk, memory module, Wi-Fi packet and QR code you use has error checking built in. Without it, a single cosmic ray hitting a RAM chip could silently corrupt your bank balance.",
      "practice": "Construct the 7-bit even-parity Hamming code for the data bits 1101. Then corrupt bit 6 yourself, compute the syndrome, and show that it correctly identifies position 6."
    },
    {
      "title": "Logic Gates: The Seven Basic Gates",
      "teacher": "Everything in this unit so far has been about <em>storing</em> 0s and 1s. Now we build the things that <em>act</em> on them. And there are only seven — that's it. Seven small circuits, and from them comes every computer ever built. Think of them as the alphabet of hardware.",
      "explain": "A <strong>logic gate</strong> is a small electronic circuit that takes one or more binary inputs and produces one binary output according to a fixed rule. That rule is written as a <strong>truth table</strong>, which lists the output for every possible combination of inputs. With <em>n</em> inputs, a truth table has <strong>2^n</strong> rows.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<strong>NOT (Inverter)</strong> — one input. Output is the opposite: 0→1, 1→0. Written <code>A'</code> or <code>Ā</code>.",
        "<strong>AND</strong> — output 1 <strong>only if ALL</strong> inputs are 1. Written <code>A·B</code> or <code>AB</code>. Behaves like multiplication.",
        "<strong>OR</strong> — output 1 if <strong>ANY</strong> input is 1. Written <code>A + B</code>. Behaves like addition (but 1+1=1).",
        "<strong>NAND</strong> = NOT of AND. Output 0 <strong>only when all</strong> inputs are 1; 1 otherwise.",
        "<strong>NOR</strong> = NOT of OR. Output 1 <strong>only when all</strong> inputs are 0; 0 otherwise.",
        "<strong>XOR (Exclusive-OR)</strong> — output 1 when the inputs are <strong>different</strong>. Written <code>A ⊕ B</code>.",
        "<strong>XNOR (Exclusive-NOR)</strong> — output 1 when the inputs are the <strong>same</strong>. Also called the equivalence gate.",
        "A small circle (bubble) on a gate symbol always means 'inverted output'.",
        "XOR is the heart of the adder: it gives the SUM bit while AND gives the CARRY bit. Also, XOR with 1 flips a bit, XOR with 0 leaves it unchanged, and A ⊕ A = 0."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "AND and OR in everyday words",
          "text": "AND is strict — a lift moves only if the door is closed AND the button is pressed. Both conditions must hold. OR is generous — an alarm rings if the front door OR the back door is opened. Any one condition is enough. This is exactly how the gates behave, and remembering the sentence is easier than remembering the table."
        },
        {
          "title": "NAND and NOR are just inverted outputs",
          "text": "Do not learn NAND and NOR as new tables. Work out the AND column, then flip every entry — that is NAND. Work out the OR column, then flip every entry — that is NOR. The bubble on the symbol is the picture of that flip. This halves what you have to memorise."
        },
        {
          "title": "XOR — the 'different' detector",
          "text": "XOR outputs 1 exactly when its two inputs disagree. That makes it a comparator: if A ⊕ B = 0, the two bits are identical. XOR is used for the sum bit in adders, for parity generation, for simple encryption, and for detecting change. XNOR is its opposite — it outputs 1 when the inputs agree."
        },
        {
          "title": "The half adder, built from two gates",
          "text": "Take two bits A and B. The SUM is A ⊕ B and the CARRY is A · B. Check it: 1 + 1 gives sum 0 (since 1⊕1 = 0) and carry 1 (since 1·1 = 1) — exactly the binary addition rule from earlier. So one XOR gate plus one AND gate is a complete half adder."
        }
      ],
      "realLife": "A washing machine will only start if the lid is closed AND the start button is pressed — that is an AND gate. The buzzer sounds if the cycle is finished OR the lid is opened mid-wash — that is an OR gate. Real appliances really are built from these.",
      "table": {
        "headers": ["A", "B", "AND (A·B)", "OR (A+B)", "NAND", "NOR", "XOR (A⊕B)", "XNOR"],
        "rows": [
          ["0", "0", "0", "0", "1", "1", "0", "1"],
