      "worked": "<strong>The NOT gate (only 2 rows, since it has one input):</strong><br><code>A | A'</code><br><code>0 |&nbsp; 1</code><br><code>1 |&nbsp; 0</code><br><br><strong>Reading the big table above, row by row:</strong><br>Row A=0, B=0 → AND needs both 1s, so 0. OR needs any 1, so 0. NAND = flip AND = 1. NOR = flip OR = 1. Inputs are the SAME, so XOR = 0 and XNOR = 1.<br>Row A=0, B=1 → AND = 0, OR = 1, NAND = 1, NOR = 0. Inputs DIFFER, so XOR = 1, XNOR = 0.<br>Row A=1, B=0 → identical to the row above by symmetry: 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0.<br>Row A=1, B=1 → AND = 1, OR = 1, NAND = 0, NOR = 0. Inputs are the SAME, so XOR = 0, XNOR = 1.<br><br><strong>A worked circuit: evaluate F = (A · B)' + C for A=1, B=1, C=0.</strong><br>Step 1: A · B = 1 · 1 = 1<br>Step 2: (A · B)' = 1' = 0<br>Step 3: F = 0 + C = 0 + 0 = <strong>0</strong><br>Now try A=1, B=0, C=0: A·B = 0, its complement = 1, F = 1 + 0 = <strong>1</strong>.<br><br><strong>Half adder check — add 1 + 1:</strong><br>SUM = A ⊕ B = 1 ⊕ 1 = <strong>0</strong><br>CARRY = A · B = 1 · 1 = <strong>1</strong><br>Result 10 in binary = 2 in decimal ✓ — exactly what 1 + 1 should give.",
      "mistake": "In Boolean algebra <strong>1 + 1 = 1</strong>, not 2 and not 10. The '+' symbol means OR here, not arithmetic addition. Students who have just finished the binary arithmetic topic get caught by this every single year — watch for which context you are in.",
      "examAnswer": "A logic gate is an electronic circuit that performs a basic logical operation on one or more binary inputs and produces a single binary output. The NOT gate, or inverter, has one input and produces its complement. The AND gate produces an output of 1 only when all its inputs are 1, while the OR gate produces an output of 1 when at least one input is 1. The NAND and NOR gates produce the complements of the AND and OR outputs respectively. The Exclusive-OR gate produces an output of 1 when its inputs are unequal, and the Exclusive-NOR gate produces an output of 1 when its inputs are equal. The behaviour of each gate is completely specified by its truth table, which lists the output for all 2^n combinations of n inputs.",
      "remember": "AND = all. OR = any. N in front = flip the answer. X = different (XOR) or same (XNOR).",
      "why": "Every adder, multiplier, memory cell and control circuit inside a CPU is built entirely from these seven gates. Nothing else is needed — this really is the complete alphabet of hardware.",
