    {
      "title": "Boolean Algebra, De Morgan's Theorems and Universal Gates",
      "teacher": "Two circuits can look totally different on paper and yet do the same job — one with nine gates, one with three. Boolean algebra is the set of rules that lets you shrink the nine down to three. Fewer gates means cheaper, faster, cooler hardware. Real money rides on this topic.",
      "explain": "<strong>Boolean algebra</strong> is the mathematics of variables that can only be 0 or 1, with three operations: AND (·), OR (+) and NOT ('). Its laws let you simplify an expression without changing its truth table. <strong>De Morgan's theorems</strong> are the two rules for pushing a NOT across a bracket, and they are what make NAND and NOR <strong>universal gates</strong> — gates from which any other gate can be built.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<strong>Identity:</strong> A + 0 = A, A · 1 = A. &nbsp; <strong>Null:</strong> A + 1 = 1, A · 0 = 0",
        "<strong>Idempotent:</strong> A + A = A, A · A = A. &nbsp; <strong>Complement:</strong> A + A' = 1, A · A' = 0. &nbsp; <strong>Double negation:</strong> (A')' = A",
        "<strong>Commutative:</strong> A + B = B + A, A·B = B·A. &nbsp; <strong>Associative:</strong> A + (B + C) = (A + B) + C, A(BC) = (AB)C",
        "<strong>Distributive:</strong> A(B + C) = AB + AC &nbsp;·&nbsp; A + BC = (A + B)(A + C)",
        "<strong>Absorption:</strong> A + AB = A &nbsp;·&nbsp; A(A + B) = A",
        "<strong>Redundancy:</strong> A + A'B = A + B &nbsp;·&nbsp; A(A' + B) = AB",
        "<strong>De Morgan 1:</strong> <code>(A · B)' = A' + B'</code> — NOT of AND is OR of NOTs.",
        "<strong>De Morgan 2:</strong> <code>(A + B)' = A' · B'</code> — NOT of OR is AND of NOTs.",
        "<strong>NAND and NOR are universal</strong> — every other gate can be built from copies of just one of them."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "De Morgan in plain English",
          "text": "'It is NOT true that both are tall' means 'at least one of them is not tall'. That is (A·B)' = A' + B'. And 'It is NOT true that either is tall' means 'both of them are not tall'. That is (A+B)' = A'·B'. The mechanical rule to remember: <strong>break the bar, change the sign</strong> — the NOT splits onto each variable, and AND swaps with OR."
        },
        {
          "title": "Why NAND is universal",
          "text": "NOT: tie both NAND inputs together — A NAND A = (A·A)' = A'. AND: take a NAND and invert its output with another NAND — (A NAND B) NAND (A NAND B) = A·B. OR: invert both inputs first — A' NAND B' = (A'·B')' = A + B, by De Morgan. Since NOT, AND and OR can all be built, and those three can build anything, NAND alone is enough for every digital circuit."
        },
        {
          "title": "Why NOR is universal too",
          "text": "By the mirror argument. NOT: A NOR A = (A+A)' = A'. OR: a NOR followed by an inverting NOR gives A + B. AND: invert both inputs first — A' NOR B' = (A'+B')' = A·B, again by De Morgan. So NOR alone is also complete."
        },
        {
          "title": "Why factories actually care",
          "text": "A NAND gate needs fewer transistors than an AND gate in CMOS technology, because AND is physically built as NAND followed by an inverter. So using only NAND gates means fewer transistors, less silicon area, lower cost and less heat. The universality of NAND is not just a maths curiosity — it is how real chips get manufactured."
        }
      ],
      "realLife": "Think of a shop that stocks only one type of Lego brick but can still build any model. NAND is that brick. Chip factories keep one highly optimised NAND cell and build entire processors from repeated copies of it.",
      "table": {
        "headers": ["Gate to build", "Using only NAND gates", "Using only NOR gates"],
        "rows": [
          ["NOT", "A NAND A", "A NOR A"],
          ["AND", "(A NAND B) NAND (A NAND B)", "(A NOR A) NOR (B NOR B)"],
          ["OR", "(A NAND A) NAND (B NAND B)", "(A NOR B) NOR (A NOR B)"],
          ["Gates needed for AND", "2", "3"],
          ["Gates needed for OR", "3", "2"]
        ]
      },
      "worked": "<strong>1. Verify De Morgan's first theorem: (A·B)' = A' + B'</strong><br><code>A B | A·B | (A·B)' | A' | B' | A'+B'</code><br><code>0 0 |&nbsp; 0&nbsp; |&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp; 1 |&nbsp; 1 |&nbsp;&nbsp; 1</code><br><code>0 1 |&nbsp; 0&nbsp; |&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp; 1 |&nbsp; 0 |&nbsp;&nbsp; 1</code><br><code>1 0 |&nbsp; 0&nbsp; |&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp; 0 |&nbsp; 1 |&nbsp;&nbsp; 1</code><br><code>1 1 |&nbsp; 1&nbsp; |&nbsp;&nbsp; 0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp; 0 |&nbsp; 0 |&nbsp;&nbsp; 0</code><br>Column 4 and column 7 are identical in every row → the theorem holds ✓<br><br><strong>2. Simplify F = AB + AB' + A'B using the laws.</strong><br>F = AB + AB' + A'B<br>= A(B + B') + A'B &nbsp;&nbsp; <em>(distributive, factor out A)</em><br>= A · 1 + A'B &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <em>(complement law: B + B' = 1)</em><br>= A + A'B &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <em>(identity law: A · 1 = A)</em><br>= <strong>A + B</strong> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <em>(redundancy law: A + A'B = A + B)</em><br>Three product terms became one OR gate.<br><em>Check with a truth table:</em> the original is 1 for AB(11), AB'(10) and A'B(01) — that is every row except 00. And A + B is also 1 everywhere except 00 ✓<br><br><strong>3. Build an OR gate using only NAND gates.</strong><br>Step 1: invert A → A NAND A = A'<br>Step 2: invert B → B NAND B = B'<br>Step 3: feed both into a third NAND → (A' · B')'<br>By De Morgan's second theorem, (A' · B')' = (A')' + (B')' = <strong>A + B</strong> ✓<br>So three NAND gates make one OR gate.<br><br><strong>4. Apply De Morgan to a longer expression: simplify (A + B·C)'</strong><br>(A + BC)' = A' · (BC)' &nbsp;&nbsp; <em>(De Morgan 2)</em><br>= A' · (B' + C') &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <em>(De Morgan 1 on the inner bracket)</em><br>= <strong>A'B' + A'C'</strong> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <em>(distributive)</em>",
      "mistake": "The most common error is writing (A·B)' = A'·B'. That is wrong — the operator must <strong>change</strong> when the bar breaks. AND becomes OR and OR becomes AND. Say it out loud every time: 'break the bar, change the sign'.",
      "examAnswer": "Boolean algebra is an algebraic system operating on variables that take only the values 0 and 1, using the operations AND, OR and NOT. Its principal laws include the identity, null, idempotent, complement, commutative, associative, distributive and absorption laws, and these are used to simplify logical expressions without altering their truth tables. De Morgan's theorems state that the complement of a product equals the sum of the complements, that is (A·B)' = A' + B', and that the complement of a sum equals the product of the complements, that is (A + B)' = A'·B'. These theorems establish that the NAND and NOR gates are universal gates, since the NOT, AND and OR functions can each be realised using only NAND gates or only NOR gates. This property is of great practical importance because it allows an entire digital system to be fabricated from a single repeated gate type, reducing manufacturing cost and complexity.",
      "remember": "De Morgan: break the bar, change the sign. NAND and NOR are universal — one brick builds everything.",
      "why": "Simplification directly reduces the number of transistors on a chip. Fewer gates means a cheaper, faster, cooler processor — and in the exam it is usually a full 7-mark question.",
      "practice": "Prove De Morgan's second theorem (A + B)' = A'·B' with a truth table. Then simplify F = A'B'C + A'BC + AB'C using Boolean laws, and build a NOT gate and an AND gate using only NOR gates."
    },
    {
      "title": "SOP, POS and Karnaugh Map Minimisation",
      "teacher": "Boolean laws work, but you have to <em>spot</em> which law to use, and under exam pressure that is hard. The Karnaugh map turns the whole thing into something you can see with your eyes — draw a grid, circle the groups, read off the answer. Honestly, it feels like cheating. Let's learn it properly.",
      "explain": "A <strong>minterm</strong> is an AND term containing every variable, corresponding to one row where the function is 1; the <strong>SOP (Sum of Products)</strong> form is the OR of all minterms. A <strong>maxterm</strong> corresponds to a row where the function is 0, and the <strong>POS (Product of Sums)</strong> form is the AND of all maxterms. A <strong>Karnaugh map</strong> is a grid arrangement of the truth table in which physically adjacent cells differ by exactly one variable, so circling adjacent 1s automatically cancels variables and gives the minimal expression.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<strong>Minterm</strong>: variable plain if its bit is 1, complemented if 0 — row A=1,B=0,C=1 gives <code>AB'C</code>. <strong>Maxterm</strong>: the opposite rule — row A=1,B=0,C=0 gives <code>(A' + B + C)</code>.",
        "<strong>SOP</strong> = OR of the minterms where F = 1, written <code>F = Σm(...)</code>. <strong>POS</strong> = AND of the maxterms where F = 0, written <code>F = ΠM(...)</code>. Both describe exactly the same function.",
        "A K-map for <em>n</em> variables has <strong>2^n cells</strong>: 4 cells for 2 variables, 8 for 3, 16 for 4.",
        "Row and column labels must be in <strong>Gray code order</strong> (00, 01, 11, 10) — this is what makes neighbours differ by one bit.",
        "<strong>Rule 1:</strong> group sizes must be powers of 2 — 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, never 3, 5 or 6. <strong>Rule 2:</strong> groups must be rectangular and contain only 1s (for SOP).",
        "<strong>Rule 3:</strong> make each group as <strong>large as possible</strong> — bigger groups remove more variables. <strong>Rule 4:</strong> groups <strong>may overlap</strong>, and every 1 must be in at least one group.",
        "<strong>Rule 5:</strong> the map <strong>wraps around</strong> — left edge touches right edge, top touches bottom, and the four corners are neighbours.",
        "A group of 2 removes 1 variable, a group of 4 removes 2, a group of 8 removes 3.",
        "<strong>Don't-care</strong> conditions (marked X) may be included in a group if that makes it bigger, or ignored if not."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "How to write SOP and POS from a truth table",
          "text": "Go down the output column. For every row where F = 1, write a minterm and OR them all together — that is the SOP. For every row where F = 0, write a maxterm and AND them all together — that is the POS. Both describe exactly the same function. SOP is usually preferred because it maps directly onto AND gates feeding one OR gate."
        },
        {
          "title": "Why the Gray-code ordering is essential",
          "text": "The columns are labelled 00, 01, 11, 10 — <em>not</em> 00, 01, 10, 11. In Gray order, moving one step sideways changes exactly one variable. That is the whole engine of the method: when two adjacent cells are both 1, the one variable that differs between them appears once as X and once as X', and since X + X' = 1 it simply disappears. Use ordinary binary order and the map stops working."
        },
        {
          "title": "The grouping procedure, step by step",
          "text": "First circle any 1 that has no neighbours at all (an isolated 1) — it stays as a full minterm. Then find the largest possible groups of 8, then 4, then 2, remembering the wrap-around. Keep going until every 1 is inside at least one group. Finally, discard any group whose 1s are all already covered by other groups — that group is redundant."
        },
        {
          "title": "Reading a group to get its term",
          "text": "Inside one group, look at each variable in turn. If the variable is 0 in every cell of the group, write it complemented. If it is 1 in every cell, write it plain. If it changes within the group, <strong>drop it entirely</strong>. AND together whatever survives — that is the group's product term. Then OR all the group terms to get the final SOP expression."
        }
      ],
      "realLife": "Designing the circuit that decides when a lift door may open: inputs are 'lift stopped', 'at a floor', 'no obstacle', 'button pressed'. That is a 4-variable Boolean function, and a K-map is exactly how an engineer reduces it to the smallest safe circuit.",
      "worked": "<strong>Part 1 — Write SOP and POS for a 3-variable function.</strong><br>Take F(A,B,C) = Σm(1, 3, 5, 7), meaning F = 1 for rows 1, 3, 5 and 7.<br>Row 1 = 001 → minterm <code>A'B'C</code><br>Row 3 = 011 → minterm <code>A'BC</code><br>Row 5 = 101 → minterm <code>AB'C</code><br>Row 7 = 111 → minterm <code>ABC</code><br><strong>SOP:</strong> F = A'B'C + A'BC + AB'C + ABC<br>F is 0 in rows 0, 2, 4, 6, so the <strong>POS</strong> is F = (A+B+C)(A+B'+C)(A'+B+C)(A'+B'+C)<br>Notice every minterm contains C plain, and every maxterm contains C plain — so both simplify to just <strong>F = C</strong>.<br><br><strong>Part 2 — A full 3-variable K-map: simplify F(A,B,C) = Σm(0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 7).</strong><br><br><strong>Step 1 — draw the grid.</strong> One row per value of A, columns labelled BC in <em>Gray order</em> 00, 01, 11, 10:<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;BC=00&nbsp;&nbsp;BC=01&nbsp;&nbsp;BC=11&nbsp;&nbsp;BC=10</code><br><code>A=0 |&nbsp;&nbsp; m0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;m1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;m3&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;m2</code><br><code>A=1 |&nbsp;&nbsp; m4&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;m5&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;m7&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;m6</code><br><br><strong>Step 2 — fill in the 1s</strong> for minterms 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 7:<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;BC=00&nbsp;&nbsp;BC=01&nbsp;&nbsp;BC=11&nbsp;&nbsp;BC=10</code><br><code>A=0 |&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1</code><br><code>A=1 |&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0</code><br><br><strong>Step 3 — form the largest possible groups.</strong><br><em>Group 1 (a quad):</em> the entire top row — cells m0, m1, m3, m2.<br>In this group A = 0 in every cell, so A survives as <code>A'</code>. B changes (0,0,1,1) so B is dropped. C changes (0,1,1,0) so C is dropped.<br>→ Group 1 gives the term <strong>A'</strong><br><br><em>Group 2 (a quad):</em> the two middle columns BC=01 and BC=11, across both rows — cells m1, m3, m5, m7.<br>Here A changes (0 and 1) so A is dropped. B changes (0 and 1) so B is dropped. C = 1 in all four cells, so C survives plain.<br>→ Group 2 gives the term <strong>C</strong><br><br><strong>Step 4 — check every 1 is covered.</strong> m0 ✓ and m2 ✓ by Group 1; m1 and m3 by both groups (overlap is allowed); m5 ✓ and m7 ✓ by Group 2. All six 1s covered.<br><br><strong>Step 5 — OR the group terms:</strong><br><strong>F = A' + C</strong><br><br><strong>Verification.</strong> The original SOP had six product terms of three variables each. The answer has one NOT gate and one OR gate. Test the two rows where F should be 0: m4 = (A=1, B=0, C=0) → A' = 0 and C = 0 → F = 0 ✓. m6 = (A=1, B=1, C=0) → A' = 0 and C = 0 → F = 0 ✓. Every other row gives 1 ✓<br><br><strong>Part 3 — a 4-variable map: simplify F(A,B,C,D) = Σm(0, 1, 2, 5, 8, 9, 10).</strong><br>Rows are AB in Gray order 00, 01, 11, 10; columns are CD in Gray order 00, 01, 11, 10.<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;CD=00&nbsp;&nbsp;CD=01&nbsp;&nbsp;CD=11&nbsp;&nbsp;CD=10</code><br><code>AB=00 |&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1</code>&nbsp;&nbsp;(m0, m1, m3, m2)<br><code>AB=01 |&nbsp;&nbsp; 0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0</code>&nbsp;&nbsp;(m4, m5, m7, m6)<br><code>AB=11 |&nbsp;&nbsp; 0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0</code>&nbsp;&nbsp;(m12, m13, m15, m14)<br><code>AB=10 |&nbsp;&nbsp; 1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1</code>&nbsp;&nbsp;(m8, m9, m11, m10)<br><br><em>Group 1 (quad):</em> m0, m1, m8, m9 — the first two columns of the top and bottom rows, joined by the <strong>vertical wrap-around</strong>. Here B = 0 and C = 0 throughout, while A and D change → term <strong>B'C'</strong><br><em>Group 2 (quad):</em> m0, m2, m8, m10 — the first and last columns of the top and bottom rows, joined by <strong>both</strong> wrap-arounds (these are the four corners). Here B = 0 and D = 0 throughout → term <strong>B'D'</strong><br><em>Group 3 (pair):</em> m1, m5 — CD=01 in rows AB=00 and AB=01. Here A = 0, C = 0, D = 1, while B changes → term <strong>A'C'D</strong><br><br>All seven 1s are covered: m0, m1, m8, m9 by Group 1; m2, m10 by Group 2; m5 by Group 3.<br><strong>F = B'C' + B'D' + A'C'D</strong>",
      "mistake": "Three killers. (1) Labelling the columns 00, 01, <strong>10, 11</strong> instead of Gray order 00, 01, <strong>11, 10</strong> — this breaks adjacency and every answer after it is wrong. (2) Making a group of 3 or 6 cells; group sizes must be powers of 2. (3) Forgetting the wrap-around, so the four corner cells are never grouped together — that costs an entire simplification step.",
      "examAnswer": "A Boolean function may be expressed in canonical Sum of Products form as the logical OR of the minterms corresponding to the input combinations for which the function is 1, or in canonical Product of Sums form as the logical AND of the maxterms corresponding to the combinations for which the function is 0. The Karnaugh map is a graphical method of minimising such functions, in which the truth table is arranged as a grid of 2^n cells whose row and column labels follow Gray code order, so that adjacent cells differ in exactly one variable. Minimisation is performed by grouping adjacent cells containing 1 into rectangular groups whose sizes are powers of two, making each group as large as possible, allowing groups to overlap and treating opposite edges of the map as adjacent. Within each group, any variable that changes value is eliminated and the remaining variables form a product term; the logical sum of these product terms is the minimal Sum of Products expression. A group of two cells eliminates one variable, a group of four eliminates two, and a group of eight eliminates three.",
      "remember": "Gray order labels. Groups are powers of 2. Bigger is better. Overlap is allowed. Edges wrap. Variable changes inside the group → delete it.",
      "why": "This is almost always the longest question in the digital-logic section of the paper, and it is the most reliably scorable — the method is mechanical, so with practice you get full marks every time.",
      "practice": "Simplify F(A,B,C,D) = Σm(0, 2, 5, 7, 8, 10, 13, 15) using a 4-variable K-map. Draw the grid, mark the 1s, show every group you form and write the final minimised SOP expression."
    }
